# Store all text as LF and check it out as LF on every platform.
#
# Without this, a Windows checkout with core.autocrlf=true materialises CRLF in
# the working tree. That breaks scripts/install.sh outright (a CR in
# "#!/bin/bash" / "set -e" makes bash fail at line 2) and makes the working
# tree disagree with the committed blobs. Pinning LF keeps Windows, Linux and
# CI byte-identical.
#
# No binary files are tracked in this repository, so every pattern below is
# text. The list is explicit rather than a blanket "* text=auto" so that a
# binary added later cannot be silently mangled.

# Rust: rustfmt writes LF and `cargo fmt --check` must agree.
*.rs       text eol=lf

# Shell: a CR in the shebang or in `set -e` aborts the script.
*.sh       text eol=lf

# Other text formats committed here; LF is portable and keeps the tree uniform.
*.md       text eol=lf
*.yml      text eol=lf
*.yaml     text eol=lf
*.toml     text eol=lf
*.lock     text eol=lf
*.jsonc    text eol=lf
*.example  text eol=lf

# Extension-less text files.
.gitignore      text eol=lf
.gitattributes  text eol=lf
.dockerignore   text eol=lf
Dockerfile      text eol=lf
LICENSE         text eol=lf

# Deliberately NOT pinned: *.ps1. scripts/install.ps1 and scripts/update.ps1
# declare "#Requires -Version 5.1", and CRLF is the safe convention for
# Windows PowerShell; they are never run from a Unix checkout. core.autocrlf
# keeps them CRLF on Windows, which is what we want.
